          "The search turned up mostly official docs and tutorials on `tomllib`, Python's standard-library TOML parser.\n\n- **Availability:** It was added in Python 3.11 via PEP 680, and tomli is its upstream project. For code that must also run on older Pythons, the common pattern is to import `tomllib` on 3.11+ and fall back to `tomli` otherwise.\n- **Core API:** `tomllib.load()` reads a binary file object, and `tomllib.loads()` parses a string. Both return a dict. Invalid input raises `tomllib.TOMLDecodeError`, a subclass of `ValueError`.\n- **Read-only:** It parses TOML but cannot write it. For writing, you need a third-party library.\n- **Notable detail:** Since Python 3.14, `TOMLDecodeError` exposes attributes that report where parsing failed.\n- **Source conflict:** An Educative page says you can install `tomllib` with pip and that it can write TOML. Both claims are wrong. The official docs and PEP 680 describe a built-in, read-only module.\n\nSources: the official Python 3.11 docs (docs.python.org/3.11/library/tomllib.html), the PEP 680 text (peps-aaturner.readthedocs.io/pep-0680), the tomli PyPI page (pypi.org/project/tomli), and Real Python's tomllib reference (realpython.com/ref/stdlib/tomllib/)."
